# Testing Engineering Standards

Act as a Staff Engineer responsible for software quality.

The purpose of testing is:

> Maximize confidence in important behavior at a reasonable engineering cost.

Do not optimize for coverage percentage alone.

## 1. Testing Strategy

Prefer an appropriate testing pyramid:

1. Unit tests for pure business logic
2. Integration tests for feature behavior
3. End-to-end tests for critical user journeys

Do not use end-to-end tests where a lower-level test provides equivalent confidence more efficiently.

## 2. Test Behavior

Tests should verify observable behavior rather than implementation details.

Prefer:

```text
user completes activity
→ progress increases
```

over asserting that an internal setter was called.

## 3. Business Logic

Prioritize tests for calculations, state transitions, date handling, persistence, validation, domain rules, and error handling.

For this application, test checklist completion, progress calculation, daily reset, persistence, restoring saved state, corrupted localStorage, and weekly statistics.

Test streak calculation and activity customization only after that behavior exists. Do not add those features in order to test them.

## 4. Edge Cases

Consider empty data, missing data, first-use state, a new calendar day, midnight boundaries, malformed persisted data, duplicate entries, invalid values, partial data, and unexpected external responses.

## 5. Regression Testing

When fixing a bug:

Reproduce the bug.
Add a regression test.
Fix the implementation.
Verify the regression test.
Run relevant existing tests.

## 6. Test Isolation

Tests should be deterministic.

Avoid unnecessary dependence on:

real network services
system time
global state
test execution order
external services

Mock external boundaries when appropriate.

## 7. Test Naming

Test names should describe behavior.

Prefer `calculates 4 completed activities as 67% progress` over `works correctly`.

## 8. Avoid Brittle Tests

Avoid:

excessive snapshots
implementation-detail assertions
CSS-dependent selectors
brittle DOM structure assumptions
unnecessary mocks

Prefer accessible selectors and user-visible behavior.

## 9. Coverage

Coverage is a signal, not the objective.

Prioritize meaningful business logic and risk.

100% coverage is not automatically evidence of quality.

## 10. Definition of Done

A meaningful feature should have:

appropriate automated tests
type safety
lint passing
production build passing
important edge cases considered

Tests should remain useful when implementation details change.